<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Dhabi Investment Authority</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ADIA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abu Dhabi Investment Authority is the sovereign wealth fund of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Abu Dhabi, founded in 1976 to invest the surplus revenues of its oil exports abroad. It manages a diversified global portfolio of shares, bonds, property, infrastructure and other assets on behalf of the government of Abu Dhabi, and it is consistently ranked among the largest sovereign wealth funds in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-dhabi-investment-authority.jpg" alt="Abu Dhabi Investment Authority" width="320" height="106" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Dhabi_Investment_Authority_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1967 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Abu Dhabi created the Financial Investments Board, which worked within its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Finance and managed the emirate's surplus oil revenues. In 1976 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan">Sheikh Zayed</a> bin Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan">Al Nahyan</a>, the founding president of the United Arab Emirates, turned it into the Abu Dhabi Investment Authority, with the aim of investing the government's surpluses across several classes of assets at low risk. At the time it was unusual for a government to invest its reserves in anything other than gold or short-term credit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the scandal of the Bank of Credit and Commerce International in the 1990s the fund reportedly lost hundreds of millions of dollars. In 2008 it co-chaired the International Working Group of 26 sovereign wealth funds that drew up the Generally Accepted Principles and Practices, known as the Santiago Principles, meant to show that such funds had sound governance and invested only on economic and financial grounds. It is a member of the International Forum of Sovereign Wealth Funds. Its operations have been characterised as secretive and opaque.</p><p>Several investments made at the market peaks of 2007 and 2008 lost much of their value, among them a $7 billion stake in Citigroup, which by 26 November 2009 had lost about 90 percent. Later purchases included 21.4 percent of Pension Insurance Corporation Group in 2018, a share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco">Saudi Aramco</a>'s initial public offering in 2019, a minority stake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Jio Platforms in 2020 and a minority stake in Fisher Investments with Advent International, completed in January 2025.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The fund invests in equities, fixed income and treasury, infrastructure, real estate, private equity and alternatives such as hedge funds. Its portfolio is divided into sub-funds by class of asset, each with its own managers and analysts, and in 2006 between 70 and 80 percent of its assets were managed by outside firms. It is a major buyer of U.S. institutional real estate, and its subsidiary Tawreed Investments holds Australian infrastructure such as Queensland Motorways, TransGrid and WestConnex. Board members are appointed by Emiri decree for renewable terms of three years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Dhabi Investment Authority” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Abu Dhabi Investment Authority</b>, founded in 1976, became one of the largest sovereign wealth funds in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
